@*
    The empire as the game shows it: its leader standing in a room, with their own world through the
    window behind them.

    Built the way the game builds it rather than as one picture, because it cannot be one. The world
    is a sky with bands of landscape in front of it, and the empire's city is painted between those
    bands so the hills sit behind one row of towers and in front of another. Every combination of
    room, world and city would be thousands of images; stacked, it is a few dozen.

    The numbers are the game's own, from gfx/portraits/portraits/00_portraits_main.txt: a room is 952
    by 340, the world hangs in it at seven tenths scale from the upper right, twenty-three down, and
    the character stands at the lower left, seventy-five in.
*@

<div class="sem-room @Class">
    @*
        The world first, because the room is painted over it: a room's artwork has its window cut
        out of it — a third of every one of them is transparent — and the view shows through. Placed
        as a fraction of the room so it keeps its position at whatever size the page draws this.
    *@
    <div class="world" style="@WorldStyle">
        @foreach (var layer in Backdrop)
        {
            <img src="@Session.Data.AssetUrl(layer)" alt="" loading="lazy">
        }
    </div>

    @if (Room?.Image is { Length: > 0 } room)
    {
        <img class="room" src="@Session.Data.AssetUrl(room)" alt="" loading="lazy">
    }

    @*
        The flag on the wall, painted after the room because the room is the wall.

        The game hangs one in more than one place and not the same way twice, so this is a choice
        between two of its own answers. Taken from interface/diplomacy_view.gui, the empire's
        dossier, which holds a room in a 964 by 350 container at (17, 45) and puts a flag at
        (30, 235) — thirteen in from the room's left edge and a hundred and ninety down, well clear
        of the figure, whose middle is at 362.5.

        The other answer is game_setup/species.gui, the screen where an empire is actually made: a
        smaller flag at (370, 50) of a 468 by 180 window, upper right, over the figure's shoulder.
        Nearer to what this scene is a picture of, and it looks worse — the flag ends up floating in
        the view through the window rather than hanging on anything. The wall was chosen by eye.
    *@
    @if (Flag is not null)
    {
        <div class="banner" style="@BannerStyle">
            <FlagPreview Flag="@Flag" />
        </div>
    }

    @if (Portrait is { Length: > 0 } portrait)
    {
        <img class="figure" style="@FigureStyle" src="@Session.Data.AssetUrl(portrait)"
             alt="@Alt" loading="lazy">
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The game's own room frame, in pixels.</summary>
    private const double RoomWidth = 952;

    private const double RoomHeight = 340;

    /// <summary>The world's own frame, and where the room hangs it.</summary>
    private const double WorldWidth = 800;

    private const double WorldHeight = 400;

    private const double WorldScale = 0.714;

    /// <summary>
    /// How far below the room's ceiling the world hangs.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The game writes <c>y = -23</c> against an <c>upper_right</c> anchor, which reads as
    /// twenty-three upwards; it is not. Hung upwards the world's bottom edge stops short of the
    /// window and leaves a strip of nothing along the sill of every room, and hung downwards it
    /// fills the window exactly. The artwork settles it, so the sign is taken from the artwork.
    /// </remarks>
    private const double WorldTop = 23;

    /// <summary>The game's character frame, and how far in from the left edge it stands.</summary>
    private const double FigureFrameWidth = 575;

    private const double FigureFrameHeight = 380;

    private const double FigureInset = 75;

    /// <summary>
    /// How tall the figure is drawn against the room.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The game composites a character into a room at its own scale, but its portrait frame shows
    /// 15.83 units of model where ours shows 19.65 — ours is taller so that nothing is cut off the
    /// top. Drawn at the game's scale the figure would stand too small by exactly that ratio, so it
    /// is drawn larger by exactly that ratio. What that puts above the room's ceiling is clipped,
    /// as the game clips its own frame's top forty pixels.
    /// </remarks>
    private const double FigureHeight = FigureFrameHeight / RoomHeight * (19.65 / 15.833);

    /// <summary>
    /// Where the figure's middle goes.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Not the frame's left edge, which is what the game gives, because our frame is not the game's
    /// width: showing more of the model vertically at the same picture shape shows more of it
    /// horizontally too. What both frames agree on is the model's own origin, which each puts at its
    /// centre — so the centres are lined up and the browser works the width out from the picture.
    /// Aligning the left edges instead stood every species some sixty pixels too far right.
    /// </remarks>
    private const double FigureCentre = FigureInset + (FigureFrameWidth / 2);

    [CascadingParameter]
    public required DesignSession Session { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The room the empire's leader stands in.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RoomDefinition? Room { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The world seen through the window.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public PlanetClassDefinition? World { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The empire's own city, built on that world.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public GraphicalCultureDefinition? City { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The leader's portrait, already drawn.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Portrait { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The empire's flag, hung on the wall.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EmpireFlag? Flag { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// How built-up the world through the window is, on the game's own nought-to-five scale.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The game's own <c>DEFAULT_CITY_POP_LEVEL</c> unless a host says otherwise, whose line in the
    /// defines is commented "Shown in empire designer". It comes to four, which draws every band the
    /// game has except the last — that one wants a world at five and is a city from edge to edge.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public int? Development { get; set; }

    private int Level => Development ?? Session.Data.Database.Defines.CityPopLevel;

    /// <summary>What to call the figure for a reader who cannot see it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string Alt { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>Extra classes, for sizing.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Class { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The world and the city interleaved, furthest from the viewer first.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// Sky, then a band of landscape, then a band of city, and so on — which is the order the game's
    /// own layout lists them in, and the reason a city cannot simply be painted on top. A world with
    /// fewer bands than the city has, or the other way about, keeps whatever is left over rather
    /// than dropping it.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Only the city bands belonging to a world of <see cref="Development"/> are drawn. Painting all
    /// of them put an ecumenopolis over every homeworld — the last band alone covers nearly half the
    /// frame — and left nothing of the planet to see.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Paired by the band each picture is rather than by its place in the list, because two worlds
    /// are missing a band: an arctic and a desert world have no second one, and matched by position
    /// their hills came out in front of the towers they belong behind.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private IEnumerable<string> Backdrop
    {
        get
        {
            if (World?.Sky is { Length: > 0 } sky)
            {
                yield return sky;
            }

            var scenery = World?.Scenery ?? [];
            var city = City?.CityLayers ?? [];

            var bands = Math.Max(
                scenery.Count > 0 ? scenery.Max(s => s.Band) : 0,
                city.Count > 0 ? city.Max(c => c.Band) : 0);

            for (var band = 1; band <= bands; band++)
            {
                if (scenery.FirstOrDefault(s => s.Band == band) is { } hills)
                {
                    yield return hills.Image;
                }

                if (city.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Band == band) is { } towers && towers.AppearsAt(Level))
                {
                    yield return towers.Image;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Where the world hangs, as fractions of the room, so it scales with it.</summary>
    private string WorldStyle =>
        $"width:{Percent(WorldWidth * WorldScale / RoomWidth)};" +
        $"height:{Percent(WorldHeight * WorldScale / RoomHeight)};" +
        $"right:0;top:{Percent(WorldTop / RoomHeight)}";

    /// <summary>
    /// The room the dossier measures its flag against, which is near enough ours to use directly.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// 964 by 350 against the room artwork's 952 by 340 — the same picture with its border — so
    /// unlike the creation screen's 468 by 180 window there is no proportion to convert.
    /// </remarks>
    private const double BannerFrameWidth = 964;

    private const double BannerFrameHeight = 350;

    /// <summary>
    /// The whole framed flag, border included.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// 131 rather than the 128 its sprite is named for. The name is nominal: the frame's own texture
    /// is 131 across with the coloured field 111 of it, and the frame is what the game positions.
    /// </remarks>
    private const double BannerSize = 131;

    private const double BannerLeft = 30 - 17;

    private const double BannerTop = 235 - 45;

    private string BannerStyle =>
        $"left:{Percent(BannerLeft / BannerFrameWidth)};top:{Percent(BannerTop / BannerFrameHeight)};" +
        $"width:{Percent(BannerSize / BannerFrameWidth)}";

    private string FigureStyle =>
        $"left:{Percent(FigureCentre / RoomWidth)};height:{Percent(FigureHeight)};" +
        "transform:translateX(-50%)";

    /// <summary>
    /// A fraction as a CSS percentage.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Written invariantly on purpose: a machine set to a comma decimal separator would otherwise
    /// produce a stylesheet the browser silently ignores.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string Percent(double fraction) =>
        (fraction * 100).ToString("0.####", System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) + "%";
}
